        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in District 19 (East New York, Brownsville (east), Starrett City)</h2>

  <p>District 19 is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. This means upholding both student rights and ensuring fair disciplinary practices. Students are entitled to due process, which includes being informed of the charges against them and having an opportunity to present their side of the story. Understanding the nuances of student discipline, especially for students with IEPs and 504 plans, is paramount in ensuring their rights are protected.</p>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h3>
  <p>If a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan faces disciplinary action that could lead to a suspension of more than 10 consecutive school days, or a series of suspensions that constitute a pattern of exclusion (even if no single suspension exceeds 10 days) under <a href="https://www.nysed.gov/special-education/part-201-regulations-discipline-students-disabilities" target="_blank">New York State Part 201 Regulations</a>, a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) <b>must</b> be conducted. This review determines if the student's behavior was caused by, or directly and substantially related to, their disability or a result of the school's failure to implement the IEP or 504 plan. Both prongs must be examined independently. If either is the case, the student cannot be suspended using standard disciplinary procedures and the IEP team must develop or revise a behavioral intervention plan (BIP), conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), or both.</p>

  <p><b>Understanding the Two Prongs of the MDR:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Prong 1: Was the conduct caused by, or directly and substantially related to, the child's disability?</b>  This requires the IEP team to analyze the student's behavior in light of their disability. For example, a student with ADHD may exhibit impulsivity, leading to disruptive behavior.  A student with Autism Spectrum Disorder might react negatively to sensory overload, resulting in an outburst.  The team needs to determine if the disability significantly contributed to the behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Prong 2: Was the conduct a direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</b> This is often overlooked. Did the school provide the required accommodations, modifications, and support services outlined in the IEP? For instance, if a student's IEP requires preferential seating but they were consistently placed in the back of the classroom, contributing to their disruptive behavior, the MDR should find that the behavior was a result of IEP implementation failure. The district must implement the IEP as written per <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/34/300.323" target="_blank">34 CFR §300.323</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents in District 19:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Notification:</b> You must receive written notification of the disciplinary action and the scheduled MDR meeting.  Ensure the notice includes the date, time, location, and purpose of the meeting, as well as a description of the alleged misconduct. Demand this notice immediately.</li>
    <li><b>Record Review:</b> The school must review all relevant information in the student’s file, including the IEP, any teacher observations, and any relevant information provided by the parents. You have the right to review these documents as well. Request all documents in advance of the MDR meeting under IDEA's parent participation and record access provisions.</li>
    <li><b>MDR Meeting:</b> The IEP team, including the parent(s), relevant school personnel (e.g., teacher, special education teacher, administrator), and the student (if appropriate) must meet to conduct the review. Your active participation is crucial.</li>
    <li><b>Determination:</b> The team determines whether the conduct was a manifestation of the student's disability or a result of IEP implementation failure. This must be a consensus decision of the team, not just the school’s determination.</li>
    <li><b>Outcome:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>If the behavior IS a manifestation:</b> The student returns to their placement unless the parents and the district agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the BIP. The IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and implement or revise the Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to address the behavior. If an FBA has already been conducted, the IEP team should review the FBA and modify the BIP as necessary to ensure it effectively addresses the student's needs. Under <a href="https://www.nysed.gov/special-education/responses-inquiries-discipline-students-disabilities" target="_blank">NYSED guidance</a>, the focus is on preventing the behavior from recurring.</li>
        <li><b>If the behavior is NOT a manifestation:</b> The student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, the school must continue to provide services to the student during the period of removal to the extent required under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/34/300.530" target="_blank">34 CFR §300.530</a>.  This often means providing academic instruction to allow the student to continue to participate in the general education curriculum.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Parent Rights:</b> Parents have the right to disagree with the MDR determination. If you disagree, you have the right to request a due process hearing under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). This starts the legal process to challenge the school's decision.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Common District 19 Pitfalls in MDRs & Parent Advocacy Tips:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Pre-Determined Outcomes:</b> Be wary of MDR meetings where the outcome seems predetermined. Ensure the team genuinely considers all evidence and perspectives. Insist on a thorough discussion, not just a rubber stamp.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Expertise:</b> The MDR team must have members with sufficient knowledge of the student's disability, the IEP, and relevant behavioral interventions. If the team lacks this expertise, request the participation of additional qualified individuals.</li>
    <li><b>Focus on the Behavior, Not the Disability:</b> Some districts focus solely on the severity of the behavior and fail to adequately analyze the connection to the disability. Redirect the discussion to the student's disability and how it contributes to the behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Ignoring IEP Implementation Failures:</b> Districts may be hesitant to admit they failed to implement the IEP correctly. Thoroughly document any instances of non-compliance with the IEP and present this evidence during the MDR meeting. Examples might include missed related services (speech therapy, occupational therapy), lack of accommodations in the classroom (extended time, preferential seating), or failure to implement a behavior plan consistently.</li>
    <li><b>Vague BIPs:</b> Ensure the BIP is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART). A vague BIP is ineffective.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communication with the school, including dates, times, names of individuals involved, and the content of the conversations.  This documentation is crucial if you need to pursue further action.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Suspensions in District 19: What You Need to Know</h3>
  <p>If your child is facing a suspension in District 19, it's crucial to understand your rights. First, ensure you receive written notification of the suspension and the reasons for it. Under New York State Education Law, the notice should be clear and specific, outlining the charges against your child. Attend any scheduled meetings with school officials and actively participate. Request a copy of all relevant documents and evidence, including witness statements, incident reports, and any video footage. If your child has an IEP or 504 plan, immediately request an MDR. Consider contacting a legal aid organization or advocate specializing in education law for guidance and support, especially if you believe your child's rights have been violated or the suspension is unfair. Organizations like Advocates for Children of New York (AFC) or the Legal Aid Society can provide valuable assistance. Remember to document everything, including dates, times, and names of individuals involved. Knowing your rights and advocating for your child is essential to ensuring a fair outcome. Note that short-term suspensions (5 days or fewer) still require parental notification and an opportunity to be heard, even if a formal MDR isn't triggered.</p>

  <p><b>Short-Term Suspensions vs. Long-Term Suspensions:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Short-Term Suspension (5 days or less):</b> Even for short-term suspensions, parents have the right to be informed of the reasons for the suspension and to have an opportunity to discuss the matter with school officials. The school must follow its own policies and procedures regarding short-term suspensions.</li>
    <li><b>Long-Term Suspension (more than 5 days):</b> Long-term suspensions trigger more significant due process rights, including the right to a formal hearing. For students with IEPs or 504 plans, a long-term suspension triggers the requirement for an MDR.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tips During Suspension Hearings:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Request Discovery:</b> Before the hearing, request all documents and evidence the school intends to present. This allows you to prepare a defense.</li>
    <li><b>Bring an Advocate:</b> Having an advocate or attorney present can significantly improve your chances of a favorable outcome.</li>
    <li><b>Prepare Your Testimony:</b> Practice your testimony and gather any evidence to support your child's case.</li>
    <li><b>Cross-Examine Witnesses:</b> You have the right to cross-examine witnesses presented by the school.</li>
    <li><b>Present Your Own Witnesses:</b> You can present your own witnesses to testify on your child's behalf.</li>
    <li><b>Object to Inadmissible Evidence:</b> If the school presents evidence that is irrelevant, hearsay, or otherwise inadmissible, object to its admission.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Alternatives to Suspension:</b></p>
  <p>Explore alternatives to suspension whenever possible. These alternatives may include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Restorative Justice Practices:</b> Focus on repairing harm and building relationships.</li>
    <li><b>Conflict Resolution:</b> Mediation or other conflict resolution techniques.</li>
    <li><b>Counseling:</b> Providing counseling services to address the underlying causes of the behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Behavioral Interventions:</b> Implementing or modifying the BIP.</li>
    <li><b>In-School Suspension:</b> Providing a supervised setting for students to complete their work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>FAQ: Discipline and Special Education in District 19</h3>

  <details>
    <summary><b>What is Due Process?</b></summary>
    <p>Due process, under the Fourteenth Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, guarantees fair procedures whenever the government (including public schools) seeks to deprive someone of life, liberty, or property. In the context of student discipline, it means students have the right to be notified of the charges against them and to have an opportunity to present their side of the story before being suspended or expelled. For students with disabilities, due process includes the MDR process.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary><b>What happens if I disagree with the MDR outcome?</b></summary>
    <p>If you disagree with the MDR outcome, you have the right to file a due process complaint and request a hearing. This is a formal legal process under the IDEA, and it involves presenting evidence and arguments before an impartial hearing officer. It's highly recommended to seek legal representation if you pursue a due process hearing.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary><b>My child doesn't have an IEP or 504 plan, but I suspect they have a disability. What can I do?</b></summary>
    <p>If you suspect your child has a disability, you have the right to request an evaluation from the school district. This evaluation will determine if your child is eligible for special education services. Put your request in writing and keep a copy for your records. The school must respond to your request within a reasonable timeframe. If your child is found eligible for special education, an IEP will be developed to address their needs.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
      <summary><b>What if my child's behavior is related to trauma?</b></summary>
      <p>If your child's behavior is related to trauma, it's crucial to communicate this information to the school. Trauma-informed practices recognize the impact of trauma on a child's behavior and learning. The IEP team should consider the impact of trauma when developing the IEP and BIP. The school may need to provide additional support services, such as counseling or therapy, to help your child address the trauma.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
      <summary><b>Can my child be arrested at school?</b></summary>
      <p>Yes, a student can be arrested at school if they commit a crime. However, school officials should follow proper procedures and ensure that the student's rights are protected. If your child is arrested at school, contact an attorney immediately. Special considerations apply to students with disabilities, and law enforcement should be aware of the student's IEP or 504 plan.</p>
  </details>
</body>
